    ("1. What is a family dentist, and how is it different from a regular dentist?", "A family dentist is a general dentist who provides preventive, diagnostic, and restorative care for patients of all ages, from toddlers and teens to parents and grandparents. The difference is scope and focus: a family practice deliberately offers the full range of routine services a household needs, including children's dental care, growth monitoring, and referrals to orthodontists, so every family member can be seen at the one trusted clinic. For more on the children's side of family care, see our <a href=\"/australia/childrens-dentist/\">children's dentistry guide</a>. 2"),
    ("2. When should I take my child to the family dentist for their first visit?", "Australian dentists recommend a child's first dental visit by age 1, or within 6 months of the first tooth erupting, whichever comes first. Early visits help your child build familiarity with the dental surgery, allow the dentist to check development and fluoride needs, and give parents practical advice on brushing, diet, and teething. Our <a href=\"/blog/paediatric-dentistry/\">kids' oral health journal</a> covers these milestones in more clinical detail. 3"),
    ("3. How often should the whole family have a dental check-up?", "Most families need a check-up every 6 to 12 months, depending on each person's risk of decay and gum disease. Your dentist will set a recall interval for each family member after their examination. For everyday prevention between visits, read our <a href=\"/blog/oral-health-tips/\">daily oral-health tips</a>. 4"),
    ("5. Are dental visits for the family covered by Medicare or private health insurance?", "Medicare does not cover routine dental care. However, a GP can issue a Chronic Disease Management (CDM) plan for eligible patients, providing up to $1,000 of Medicare dental cover over two years. Private health insurance extras policies typically cover 60–85% of family dental fees, and many funds offer \"gap-free\" check-ups at network dentists. For treatment cost benchmarks, see our guide to <a href=\"/blog/endodontics/\">what a root canal costs</a>. 6"),
    ("6. What treatments does a family dentist commonly provide for kids, teens, and adults?", "For kids: examinations, cleans, fluoride varnish, fissure sealants, fillings, and advice on brushing and diet. For teens: routine preventive care, sports mouthguards, and orthodontic referrals. For adults: check-ups, scale and cleans, fillings, crowns, simple root canal treatment, and extractions, plus cosmetic and implant options at some practices. Teen orthodontic needs are covered in our <a href=\"/blog/orthodontics/\">braces and clear aligners guide</a>. 7"),
    ("7. What does a family dental check-up cost in Australia, and how can I reduce the gap?", "A comprehensive examination typically costs $50–$120, and a scale and clean costs $80–$180; combined check-up and clean appointments are commonly $120–$250 in capital cities. To reduce the gap, use a \"gap-free\" dentist on your health fund's network, claim CDBS benefits for eligible children, ask for itemised quotes before treatment, and compare fees through the directories listed in this article. Our <a href=\"/australia/dentist-sydney/\">Sydney dentist listicle</a> adds suburb-level context for the city's leading family practices. 8"),
